<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valenciana mine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1760s</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as La Valenciana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Valenciana mine was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, opened by Antonio de Obregón y Alcocer in the 1760s. By the end of the eighteenth century it was the richest silver mine in the world, producing a large share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>'s output, and its owners became counts of Valenciana. The mine and its baroque church are part of the Guanajuato World Heritage Site.</p></div>
